<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>iOS UIKit Course</title><link>/docs/7.-closures/</link><description>Recent content on iOS UIKit Course</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="/docs/7.-closures/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>7.1. Closures with parameters</title><link>/docs/7.-closures/7.1.-closures-with-parameters/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/7.-closures/7.1.-closures-with-parameters/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="71-closures-with-parameters"&gt;7.1. Closures with parameters&lt;a class="anchor" href="#71-closures-with-parameters"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We can take the help of &lt;code&gt;in&lt;/code&gt; keyword to pass parameters in a closure. For example, in the following code, we define a closure to take a person&amp;rsquo;s name as a parameter and say hello to them:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; sayHelloTo = { (name:String) &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Hello &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;\(&lt;/span&gt;name&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//calling closure with parameter...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;sayHelloTo(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;Donald&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;It prints:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Hello Donald!&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;Do you find a difference between functions and closure in handling the parameter labels? :wink:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>7.2. Closures that return a value</title><link>/docs/7.-closures/7.2.-closures-that-return-a-value/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/7.-closures/7.2.-closures-that-return-a-value/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="72-closures-that-return-a-value"&gt;7.2. Closures that return a value&lt;a class="anchor" href="#72-closures-that-return-a-value"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To return a value, we would use &lt;code&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; followed by the return data type followed by &lt;code&gt;in&lt;/code&gt; keyword. For example, let&amp;rsquo;s convert the &lt;code&gt;sumOf&lt;/code&gt; function into a closure to return the sum of integers in an array.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; sumOfArray = {(array:[Int]) -&amp;gt; Int &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; sum = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; item &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; array{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; sum &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; item
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//returns the value...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; sum
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//calling the closure with an integer array&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; sum = sumOfArray([&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;])
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;print(sum)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;It prints:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>7.3. Closures as parameters</title><link>/docs/7.-closures/7.3.-closures-as-parameters/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/7.-closures/7.3.-closures-as-parameters/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="73-closures-as-parameters"&gt;7.3. Closures as parameters&lt;a class="anchor" href="#73-closures-as-parameters"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So far, we rewrote the functions as closures. So why write a closure when we can just define a function? Well, the beauty of closures is we can pass closures as parameters to a function. And iOS UIKit and SwiftUI libraries use it extensively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s define two closures, one to fly and another to drive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; drive = {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;I am driving!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; fly = {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;I am taking a flight!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Now let&amp;rsquo;s recreate a previous scenario where the user could travel from one place to another. So we can write the function as:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>7.4. Reference Code</title><link>/docs/7.-closures/7.4.-reference-code/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/7.-closures/7.4.-reference-code/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="74-reference-code"&gt;7.4. Reference Code&lt;a class="anchor" href="#74-reference-code"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
